{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YNe35wgWLdD9bwB6ZWAA4G","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-creator-0/113f7cc5-237e-5d6a-8867-a97fd89c58dd/scratchpad/e6/e6-t11/shadow/exec-ws/input-class-analysis.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"300e9ed5-f30b-4a4f-9333-f148a1e8112b","uuid":"3bee25a1-9358-49b4-b0e4-343a57dbcb7c","timestamp":"2026-08-09T17:41:44.147Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-creator-0/113f7cc5-237e-5d6a-8867-a97fd89c58dd/scratchpad/e6/e6-t11/shadow/exec-ws/input-class-analysis.md","content":"# Input-class analysis for e6-t11 (evidence requirement E-A)\n\nClaim under audit: for every ISO date `d` in the years 2023-2025 and every\n`n` with `0 <= n <= 60`, `add_days(d, n)` (in `package/artifacts/dateadd.py`)\nequals the true Gregorian calendar date `n` days after `d`.\n\nDomain size: 1096 start dates (365 + 366 + 365) x 61 values of n = 66,856\npairs. The implementation is a day-by-day increment loop driven entirely by\nthe month-length function `_dim(y, m)`, which reads the constant table\n`DAYS` (line 1) except for February in leap years (line 9-10). Therefore the\ntruth of the claim turns exactly on (a) whether every `DAYS` entry equals\nthe true month length, (b) whether the leap-year branch fires correctly for\n2024 and not for 2023/2025, and (c) whether the rollover logic (day, month,\nyear) is correct. The following partition exercises all of these.\n\n## Partition into truth-relevant input classes\n\n- **C1 — identity, n = 0.** The loop body never runs; output must equal the\n  (reformatted) input. Exercises parsing/formatting only. Example:\n  (\"2023-08-31\", 0).\n- **C2 — within-month spans.** Start day + n stays inside the start month;\n  no `DAYS` entry is compared against exceeded. Example: (\"2024-03-05\", 10).\n- **C3 — single month-boundary rollover, one class per month M = 1..12.**\n  Start at the true last day of month M, n = 1; the true result is the 1st\n  of the next month. Each class exercises exactly one `DAYS` entry (or the\n  leap branch for February). All twelve are reachable within n <= 60 from\n  start dates in 2023-2025. Critically, this class family also checks the\n  converse: for each month M, starting at day `true_len(M) - 1` with n = 1\n  must yield day `true_len(M)`, not a rollover — this is where an\n  *understated* table entry is caught.\n- **C4 — February, leap vs non-leap.** (\"2024-02-28\", 1) -> true\n  \"2024-02-29\" and (\"2024-02-29\", 1) -> true \"2024-03-01\" (leap 2024);\n  (\"2023-02-28\", 1) and (\"2025-02-28\", 1) -> true \"-03-01\" (non-leap).\n  Exercises `_leap` (2024 % 4 == 0, % 100 != 0 -> leap; 2023/2025 not\n  divisible by 4 -> non-leap; both correct by inspection of lines 4-5).\n- **C5 — year rollover.** (\"2023-12-31\", 1) -> \"2024-01-01\",\n  (\"2024-12-31\", 1) -> \"2025-01-01\", (\"2025-12-31\", 1) -> \"2026-01-01\"\n  (start dates lie in 2023-2025 as required; results may leave the range).\n  Exercises the `m > 12` reset at lines 22-24.\n- **C6 — multi-month spans, n up to 60.** A span of up to 60 days can cross\n  two month boundaries (e.g. (\"2024-01-31\", 60) crossing Jan->Feb->Mar in a\n  leap year); checks accumulation of month lengths, in particular any\n  wrong `DAYS` entry shifts every date downstream of it.\n- **C7 — spans passing through a day the table denies exists.** If a `DAYS`\n  entry is *smaller* than the true month length, every span whose true\n  result set includes the missing day(s) is shifted one day late. This class\n  is the intersection of C3/C6 with the table audit below.\n\nTogether C1-C7 exercise every `DAYS` constant (C3), the leap-year branch\nfor 2024 and its absence for 2023/2025 (C4), every month-boundary rollover\nreachable with n <= 60 (C3, C6), the year-boundary rollover inside the\ndomain (C5), the n = 0 identity (C1), and multi-month accumulation (C6).\n\n## Table audit (drives C3/C7)\n\n| Month | `DAYS` entry | true length | match |\n|-------|--------------|-------------|-------|\n| Jan   | 31 | 31 | yes |\n| Feb   | 28 (29 via leap branch in 2024) | 28/29 | yes |\n| Mar   | 31 | 31 | yes |\n| Apr   | 30 | 30 | yes |\n| May   | 31 | 31 | yes |\n| Jun   | 30 | 30 | yes |\n| Jul   | 31 | 31 | yes |\n| **Aug** | **30** | **31** | **NO** |\n| Sep   | 30 | 30 | yes |\n| Oct   | 31 | 31 | yes |\n| Nov   | 30 | 30 | yes |\n| Dec   | 31 | 31 | yes |\n\nTable sum is 364 (non-leap), one short of 365: August is recorded as 30\ndays. Every other entry, the leap logic, and the rollover logic are correct.\n\n## Witness derivation and mechanical trace (evidence requirement E-C)\n\nWitness: `(\"2023-08-30\", 1)` — in domain (date in 2023, 0 <= 1 <= 60).\n\nTrue Gregorian result: August 2023 has 31 days, so one day after\n2023-08-30 is **2023-08-31**.\n\nTrace of `add_days(\"2023-08-30\", 1)`:\n1. Parse: y=2023, m=8, d=30 (line 16).\n2. Loop iteration 1 (n=1): d -> 31 (line 18).\n3. `_dim(2023, 8)`: m != 2, return `DAYS[7]` = **30** (lines 8-11).\n4. 31 > 30, so d=1, m=9; m <= 12, no year change (lines 19-24).\n5. Return `\"2023-09-01\"` (line 25).\n\nOutput `\"2023-09-01\"` != true `\"2023-08-31\"`. The claim is **VIOLATED**;\nthe mismatch is mechanical and re-derivable by any party from the source.\n\nConsequence structure (class C7): the code skips the real date Aug 31\nentirely — every (d, n) pair whose true result is `YYYY-08-31` or whose\nspan passes through Aug 31 is wrong (shifted one day late), in each of\n2023, 2024, 2025. Spans that merely *start* on Aug 31 are unaffected\n(n=0 returns it verbatim; n>=1 leaves August immediately and happens to\nland correctly because the true offset from Aug 31 is preserved once the\nskipped day is behind the start point).\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
